# Developer Getting Started

This guide takes you from a fresh clone to a working local dev loop:
build, run, edit, test, and pass the quality gate. Read it top-to-bottom
on your first day; afterwards use the headings as a reference.

For operator install instructions see [Installing Herold](../admin/install);
for the design baseline see `docs/design/` in the repository.

## Prerequisites

| Tool | Required version | Notes |
|------|-----------------|-------|
| Go | 1.26 or newer | `go.mod` pins `go 1.26.4`; `go` fetches the pinned toolchain transparently on the first build |
| git | any recent | clone and pre-commit hooks |
| Node.js | 20 LTS or newer | only needed to build or develop the web SPAs under `web/` |
| pnpm | 10 | workspace manager for the `web/` monorepo; `npm install -g pnpm@10` |

The default build is pure Go -- no C compiler, no CGO, no shared
libraries. The `nofrontend` build tag lets you compile the backend with
no Node toolchain at all (see [Backend-only build](#backend-only-build)).

## Clone and first build

```bash
git clone https://code.netzhansa.com/hanshuebner/herold.git
cd herold
make build
```

`make build` runs `make build-web` (pnpm workspace build, produces the
Suite SPA) then `make build-server` (compiles the binary). The binary
lands at `bin/herold`.

### The prep-web step

The Go source embeds the compiled web assets via `//go:embed dist` in
`internal/webspa/`. On a fresh checkout the `dist/` tree is empty, which
causes `go build`, `go vet`, and `go test` to fail with an embed error.

`make prep-web` copies tracked placeholder files from
`internal/webspa/placeholder/` into `internal/webspa/dist/` when the
real assets are absent, so the build succeeds without a full pnpm
run. `make build-server` and `make test-server` both depend on
`prep-web` and run it automatically; you only need to invoke it
explicitly if you call `go build ./...` or `go test ./...` directly.

```bash
# Run once after a fresh clone if you are not running make build:
make prep-web
```

### Backend-only build

When only Go code has changed and the web assets are already current,
skip the pnpm step:

```bash
make build-server
```

To build the binary with no web assets at all (Go-only environment,
no Node required):

```bash
GOFLAGS="-tags nofrontend" make build-server
```

The `nofrontend` binary serves a placeholder page in place of the Suite
app. It is suitable for backend development and Go-only CI, not for
production or UI work.

## Running locally

### make dev (recommended)

`make dev` is the one-command web dev loop. It builds the server,
starts a herold backend on port 8080, and starts the Vite dev server
for the Suite SPA at `http://localhost:5173/` with hot module reload.
Editing any file under `web/apps/suite/` reloads in the browser with
no backend restart.

```bash
make dev
```

The first run provisions a self-signed admin cert, bootstraps
`admin@example.local`, and seeds principals `alice`, `filip`, and
`bob` on `example.local` (all with password `testpass123...` -- the
three trailing dots are part of the password). Subsequent runs reuse
the existing store, so your test mail and other local state survive
restarts. State lives in a gitignored `.dev/` directory; override it
with `HEROLD_DEV_DIR`.

Override the ports with `HEROLD_DEV_BACKEND_PORT` (default 8080) and
`HEROLD_DEV_VITE_PORT` (default 5173). Press Ctrl-C to stop both the
backend and Vite; the `.dev/` store is preserved.

```bash
# Run on a different port pair, leaving the reserved 8080/5173 free:
HEROLD_DEV_BACKEND_PORT=18080 HEROLD_DEV_VITE_PORT=15173 make dev
```

### Backend + Vite dev server (manual two-terminal loop)

`make dev` automates the two steps below; reach for the manual form
when you want to run the backend with your own config or point Vite at
a backend it did not start. Start a herold backend on port 8080 with
your own config, then start the Vite dev server for the Suite SPA:

```bash
# Terminal 1: backend
bin/herold server start --system-config data/system.toml

# Terminal 2: Suite SPA with hot-reload
pnpm -C web dev --filter @herold/suite
```

Vite starts at `http://localhost:5173/` and proxies the herold API
paths (`/api`, `/jmap`, `/.well-known/jmap`, `/chat/ws`, `/login`,
`/oidc`, `/proxy`) to the backend at `http://localhost:8080`. Set
`HEROLD_URL` to point Vite at a different backend address.

Ports **8080** (backend) and **5173** (Vite) are the reserved manual
session ports; automated tooling must not bind them.

For the admin SPA:

```bash
pnpm -C web dev --filter @herold/admin
```

The admin SPA starts at `http://localhost:5174/`.

### Manual documentation loop

To edit and preview the operator manual:

```bash
make manual
```

This builds the `.mdoc` sources to SSR HTML, serves them at
`http://localhost:8000/`, and live-reloads the browser on every save.
No herold binary is needed.

### Ephemeral instances on kernel-picked ports

`scripts/dev-instance.sh` provisions a complete throwaway herold
instance -- binary, SQLite store, self-signed TLS, Vite dev server --
on kernel-picked ports, so it does not collide with the manual session.
The instance is seeded with a domain and four principals and is torn
down when you kill the script.

```bash
# Start in foreground (SIGINT to tear down):
scripts/dev-instance.sh start

# The script prints one key=value per line:
#   INSTANCE=<hex id>
#   STATE_DIR=/tmp/herold-instances/<hex id>
#   BACKEND_URL=http://127.0.0.1:<port>
#   ADMIN_URL=http://127.0.0.1:<port>
#   SUITE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:<port>
#   IMAP_ADDR=127.0.0.1:<port>
#   SMTP_ADDR=127.0.0.1:<port>
#   SMTP_SUBMISSION_ADDR=127.0.0.1:<port>
```

The seed always provisions:

| Principal | Password |
|-----------|----------|
| `admin@example.local` | `testpass123...` |
| `alice@example.local` | `testpass123...` |
| `filip@example.local` | `testpass123...` |
| `bob@example.local`   | `testpass123...` |

The three trailing dots in `testpass123...` are part of the password,
not punctuation.

To stop an instance by ID:

```bash
scripts/dev-instance.sh stop <id>
```

To list running instances:

```bash
scripts/dev-instance.sh list
```

To remove tempdirs for dead instances:

```bash
scripts/dev-instance.sh gc
```

`scripts/dev-instance.sh` requires: `bash` 4+, `jq`, `lsof`, `curl`,
and the herold binary at `bin/herold` (build it with `make build-server`
first).

## Tests

### Go test suite

```bash
make test-server     # go test -race -count=1 ./...
make test-short      # same, with -short (skips slow integration paths)
```

Both targets run `make prep-web` first so the embed resolves.

### Web test suite

```bash
make test-web        # pnpm install + svelte-check + vitest + lint
```

### Run everything

```bash
make test            # runs make test-server
```

`make test` currently runs the server suite. `make test-web` is a
separate step; CI runs both.

### SQLite and Postgres

The default `make test-server` run uses SQLite. Postgres tests are
gated on the `HEROLD_PG_DSN` environment variable and skip cleanly
when it is not set:

```bash
HEROLD_PG_DSN="postgres://herold:herold@localhost:5432/herold?sslmode=disable" \
  make test-server
```

Both backends must pass. Code that works on only one backend is not
mergeable.

To start a local Postgres for testing:

```bash
docker run -d --name herold-pg --rm \
  -p 5432:5432 \
  -e POSTGRES_USER=herold \
  -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=herold \
  -e POSTGRES_DB=herold \
  postgres:16
```

### Admin SPA end-to-end suite

```bash
pnpm --filter @herold/admin test:e2e
```

Install Playwright browsers on first run:

```bash
pnpm --filter @herold/admin test:e2e:install
```

## Quality gate

### Formatting and static analysis

```bash
make lint            # gofmt check + go vet + staticcheck
```

Broken formatting fails CI. Run `make fmt` to reformat in-place, or
install goimports and let it handle import grouping:

```bash
make fmt
```

`staticcheck` must be on your PATH. Install it once:

```bash
go install honnef.co/go/tools/cmd/staticcheck@latest
```

### Schema-version invariant

Every migration file must match the `CurrentSchemaVersion` constant in
the store package. The invariant is checked in under one second:

```bash
make check-schema-version
```

This check runs automatically in the pre-commit hook.

### Pre-commit hooks

Install the pre-commit hook once per clone:

```bash
make install-hooks
```

The hook runs: `gofmt`, `goimports`, `go vet`, `go mod tidy`,
`staticcheck`, the schema-version invariant, the fast diag tests
(`internal/diag/backup`, `internal/diag/migrate`), `gitleaks`, and
generic file hygiene. `git commit` triggers it; `git push` does not.

To run the same checks manually (staged files only):

```bash
make precommit
```

Or across all files:

```bash
make precommit-all
```

### Local CI lane

```bash
make ci-local        # fmt-check + go vet + test + govulncheck
```

This is the fast subset of CI. `staticcheck` and the interop suite are
separate (`make staticcheck`, `make interop`).

`govulncheck` must be on your PATH:

```bash
go install golang.org/x/vuln/cmd/govulncheck@latest
```

### Fuzz smoke pass

```bash
make fuzz-short      # every Fuzz* target, 30 s each (FUZZTIME=30s)
```

Override the per-target budget with `FUZZTIME`:

```bash
FUZZTIME=5s make fuzz-short
```

## Where to go next

**Design baseline** -- read these before changing any subsystem:

- `docs/design/00-scope.md` -- goals, non-goals, and the target scale.
- `docs/design/server/requirements/` -- numbered requirements (`REQ-XXX-nn`)
  per subsystem. Authoritative over everything else.
- `docs/design/server/architecture/` -- how the subsystems fit together.
- `docs/design/server/implementation/` -- tech stack, phasing, testing
  strategy, simplification decisions.
- `docs/design/web/` -- web SPA scope, architecture, and ADRs.

**Working agreements**:

- `STANDARDS.md` -- coding and development rules. Authoritative over all
  other guidance. Re-read it when in doubt.
- `AGENTS.md` -- the subsystem map: which package is owned by which area,
  and which RFCs govern it.
- `web/CLAUDE.md` -- web workspace rules: build pipeline, UI verification
  requirements, patterns to avoid, and the ephemeral-instance contract.

**CI** -- the authoritative remote and CI are hosted on the Forgejo
instance at `code.netzhansa.com`. The `origin` remote points there.
GitHub is a mirror. CI runs on every push to `main` via Forgejo Actions
(`.forgejo/workflows/ci.yml`). A failure on `main` is fixed forward;
the branch is not reverted.
